Apple is considering delaying or scrapping its planned <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> with an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> display amid concerns about high panel costs and weaker-than-expected demand for &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; devices, according to <a href="https://www.thelec.net/news/articleView.html?idxno=15762"><em>The Elec</em></a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/03/imac-new-blue.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-942644" /><br/>
Citing industry sources, the Korean publication says Apple is discussing changes to the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;iMac&zwnj;'s development schedule with Samsung Display. The company could put the project on hold altogether, although one industry official said the talks are currently focused on delaying it while Apple determines demand. <br/>
<br/>
Previous reports pointed to a launch in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/04/28/major-imac-upgrade-coming-in-2029/">2029 or 2030</a>. Apple has now reportedly asked LCD suppliers, including BOE, to develop panels for a new &zwnj;iMac&zwnj; expected around 2029, suggesting it could stick with LCD technology through the period originally planned for the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; upgrade.<br/>
<br/>
Cost is said to be the biggest obstacle. Apple sells fewer iMacs than other products like iPhones or MacBooks, making expensive &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panels harder to justify without raising the computer's price. <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/02/apple-memory-costs-increase-december/">Rising memory costs</a> are reportedly adding to the expense of bringing &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; to more of Apple's products.<br/>
<br/>
Weak &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-pro/">iPad Pro</a> demand is reportedly another factor. The 2024 models introduced brighter tandem &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; displays, but higher prices appear to have discouraged many buyers. By October that year, Apple had reportedly <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/10/09/m4-ipad-pro-oled-panel-sluggish-demand/">cut its shipments</a> from as many as 10 million units to 6.7 million.<br/>
<br/>
Samsung Display and LG Display are forecast to ship a combined 4.9 million &zwnj;iPad Pro&zwnj; &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panels in 2026, down approximately 18% from six million in 2025. Samsung's shipments are expected to fall from 2.1 million to 1.5 million panels, while LG's are projected to drop from 3.9 million to 3.4 million.<br/>
<br/>
<em>The Elec</em> suggests the review may also reflect Apple's change in leadership. Since John Ternus <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/01/john-ternus-is-now-apple-ceo/">became CEO</a> in September, Apple has reportedly been reassessing whether the benefits of expensive &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panels justify their cost.<br/>
<br/>
Apple purportedly first <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/12/18/apple-developing-24-inch-oled-imac/">approached display suppliers</a> about the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;iMac&zwnj; in 2025, seeking a 24-inch panel with 600 nits of brightness and a pixel density of 218 pixels per inch.<br/>
<br/>
Samsung Display is believed to have been developing a panel at roughly 220 pixels per inch for the device on its existing large-format quantum dot &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; production lines. The design uses five light-emitting layers to improve brightness and lifespan, and Samsung had planned to send Apple samples in the second half of 2026.<br/>
<br/>
LG Display was working on a competing white &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panel with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/10/oled-imac-panel-extra-layer-screen/">five light-emitting layers</a>, but development of its panel for the first &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;iMac&zwnj; has reportedly stopped.<br/>
<br/>
